<!--
  `F-173` — the crescent inside the snooze target.

  ## Why a moon and not an X

  Messenger's target carries an X, because dropping a chat head there **closes
  the conversation**. SWIP's does not close anything — it hides the button for
  ten minutes and it comes straight back. An X would promise a destruction that
  does not happen, and the first time somebody dropped the bubble expecting it
  to be gone for good they would have been told something untrue by an icon.

  A crescent says "asleep", which is exactly what happens and is legible at
  28 dp in a way that no word is.

  ## Why the path is drawn from two curves rather than boolean-subtracted

  `VectorDrawable` has no boolean operations, so a crescent cannot be a circle
  minus a circle. It is one closed path: the outer arc runs down the left side
  from top to bottom, and the inner arc returns up through the middle, which
  leaves the lune between them filled. Approximated with cubics because
  `pathData` arcs are supported unevenly across API levels and a shape that
  silently fails to parse on older Android would be an invisible icon in a
  window the user is being asked to aim at.

  Geometry sits inside the middle 20 of a 24 unit viewport, with the fill
  hard-coded for the same reason the ring's colours are.
-->
